# Cursor Rules for app.monorepo

You are an expert full-stack engineer working on a production monorepo with the following strict rules.

## Project Structure

This is a pnpm monorepo with the following structure:

- `client/` - React frontend application
- `server/` - Express backend API
- `packages/` - Shared utilities and libraries
- `scripts/` - Build and deployment scripts

## Tech Stack

### Frontend (client/)

- React 19+ with TypeScript (strict mode, no any)
- Vite as build tool
- Tailwind CSS v4+
- shadcn/ui (latest) – always use existing components when possible
- ESLint + Prettier (@eds-open/eslint-config-bundle)
- State management: zustand
- Forms: react-hook-form + zod
- Routing: react-router-dom v6
- Hooks: ahooks for common patterns
- Jest + Testing Library for unit tests, Playwright for E2E

### Backend (server/)

- Express + TypeScript
- Node.js >=20
- Winston for logging
- Helmet for security
- CORS and compression middleware

### Package Manager

- pnpm (>=10) - NEVER use npm or yarn
- Workspace protocol for internal dependencies

## Frontend Code Style Rules (MUST FOLLOW)

### Component Structure

- Function components + hooks only (no class components ever)
- Always destructure props in the function parameters
- Use descriptive prop names (never abbreviations like txt, onClk, etc.)
- One component per file. Never multiple named exports unless they are tiny (< 30 lines each) and tightly coupled (max 3 per file)
- Keep components small and focused (React Server Components mindset)
- File names: PascalCase.tsx for components

### Styling

- Use cn() from "@/utils/cn" for all className merging
- Tailwind CSS v4 utility classes only
- No inline styles or CSS modules
- Import shadcn components from "@/components/ui/..." when available

### TypeScript

- Strict mode enabled (no any, unknown preferred)
- Explicit return types for exported functions
- Use type for props, interface for objects that may be extended
- Proper typing for event handlers and refs

### Imports

- Use @/ alias for src directory imports
- Group imports: external packages → internal modules → components → utils → types
- Named exports preferred over default exports (except for pages/routes)

### State Management & Prop Drilling Rules

- Use zustand for global state
- Keep state close to where it's used
- Use ahooks for common patterns (useRequest, useMount, etc.)
- Never prop drill more than one level (parent → child only)
- For deeper state sharing, use React Context at the common ancestor
- For global app state, use Zustand Store

### Forms

- Use react-hook-form for all forms
- Validate with zod schemas
- Use @hookform/resolvers for integration

## Backend Code Style Rules

### Structure

- Module-based organization in server/src/modules/
- Each module contains: controller, service, types
- Routes defined in server/src/routes.ts
- Middleware in server/src/middleware/

### Error Handling

- Use try-catch blocks in async functions
- Return proper HTTP status codes
- Log errors with Winston logger
- Never expose internal errors to client

### API Design

- RESTful conventions
- Consistent response format
- Proper HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE)
- Version APIs if needed (/api/v1/...)

## Code Quality Standards

### General Rules

- Modern ES6+ syntax (async/await, destructuring, spread, etc.)
- Descriptive variable and function names (no abbreviations)
- Single responsibility principle
- DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself)
- Comments only for complex logic, not obvious code
- Return typed responses

### TypeScript Rules

- No any type (use unknown if needed)
- No type assertions unless absolutely necessary
- Prefer const over let, never use var
- Use optional chaining (?.) and nullish coalescing (??)

### Testing

- Write tests when explicitly requested
- Use descriptive test names
- Test behavior, not implementation
- Place tests in `__tests__/` directories adjacent to source
- Use `describe` for grouping, `it` for individual tests
- Clear mocks in `afterEach`
- Keep each test to 2–3 assertions max
- Prefer validating objects via `toContainEqual`/`objectContaining` over asserting individual properties
- Test behavior, not implementation: Test what users see and do, not internal component details
- Avoid testing: Internal state, private methods, implementation details that could change
- Prefer testing: User interactions, rendered output, callbacks being invoked
- Skip dedicated tests for: Thin wrapper components, simple presentational components, components already covered by integration tests

## Development Workflow

### When Implementing

- Always return complete file(s) with correct imports
- No snippets or partial code unless explicitly asked
- Fix linter errors immediately after changes
- Test changes if possible

### File Operations

- NEVER create documentation files (*.md, README) unless explicitly requested
- NEVER create config files unless explicitly requested
- Prefer editing existing files over creating new ones
- Clean up temporary files after use

### Git Operations

- NEVER commit unless explicitly requested
- NEVER modify git config
- NEVER force push or destructive operations

## Browser Support

- Modern evergreen browsers only (Chrome, Firefox, Safari, Edge)
- No IE or legacy browser support needed
- Use modern JavaScript/CSS features freely

## Performance

- Code splitting for routes
- Lazy load components when appropriate
- Optimize images and assets
- Minimize bundle size

## Security

- Sanitize user inputs
- Use Helmet middleware on backend
- Proper CORS configuration
- Environment variables for secrets (never hardcode)

## Important Notes

- When implementing, focus on code quality and maintainability
- Follow existing patterns in the codebase
- Ask for clarification if requirements are unclear
